    stimulus: 'Leon Festinger proposed that people experience psychological discomfort—cognitive dissonance—when they hold beliefs or take actions that contradict each other. Crucially, this discomfort motivates efforts to reduce the inconsistency, but not necessarily by changing behavior. In one study, smokers who acknowledged that smoking caused cancer were more likely to question the scientific evidence than to quit: challenging a well-established scientific consensus was psychologically easier than abandoning an addictive behavior. More recent research extends this finding: people who have already made an irreversible decision—purchasing a car, accepting a job—consistently rate their chosen option more favorably afterward than they rated it before the decision, adjusting their attitudes to align with their behavior rather than the reverse.',
