    stimulus: 'Oral epic poetry—works like Homer\'s Iliad, the Sanskrit Mahabharata, and the South Slavic epic cycles—was composed and performed without written texts for centuries. Albert Lord and Milman Parry, studying living oral epic traditions in Yugoslavia in the 1930s, discovered that skilled oral bards did not recite memorized fixed texts but improvised each performance from within a store of traditional formulas. These formulas—prefabricated phrases such as "swift-footed Achilles" or "rosy-fingered Dawn"—were designed to fill specific metrical positions in the verse line, enabling a singer to compose metrically correct poetry at the pace of live performance without preparation. The oral-formulaic system allowed a skilled bard to sing continuously for hours while maintaining metrical and narrative coherence, without ever consulting a written text or relying on word-for-word memorization.',
