    stimulus: 'In Federalist No. 51, James Madison argued that the new constitutional framework would prevent tyranny through the structure of government itself rather than through the virtue of officeholders. Madison assumed that persons in power would tend to abuse it if unopposed. The remedy, he argued, was to construct the government so that each branch had both the constitutional means and the personal incentives to resist encroachments from the others—"ambition must be made to counteract ambition." An executive accountable to the legislature, a legislature divided against itself, and an independent judiciary providing review of both: the genius of the system lay in harnessing self-interest rather than relying on individual virtue. This principle, Madison contended, was the only reliable foundation for republican government given the inevitable imperfection of human nature.',
